{"id": "q0160", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "conservative", "tier": "conservative", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 4242962681, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "1bd884090589f25ac8e99f9bb754f4b90b4c7a09d6887616d0aefbdba25a74d4", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.9057239890098572, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 6, "query": " Consider the transition between two forms of solid tin, $\\mathrm{Sn}(s$, gray $) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Sn}(s$, white $)$. The two phases are in equilibrium at 1 bar and $18^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. The densities for gray and white tin are 5750 and $7280 \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}$, respectively, and the molar entropies for gray and white tin are 44.14 and $51.18 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$, respectively. Calculate the temperature at which the two phases are in equilibrium at 350. bar.\n\nA. 5.2 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nB. -7.8 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nC. -3.5 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nD. 0.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nE. 21.4 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nF. -10.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nG. 10.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nH. -15.2 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nI. -25.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nJ. 13.7 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0160.wav", "input_sha256": "be940326112268860e32271ab7300efe2524c9efaeb7d08c0a1020fb54e3ba81", "input_samples": 949600, "input_s": 59.35, "n_real_chunks": 60, "score": 0.8915, "act": 0.9973, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Okay, so this is a phase equilibrium problem involving gray and white tin. We are given the density of each form and their molar entropies at 18. 0 °C under 1 bar, but we need to find the temperature where they are in equi librium at 3 50 bar. Since the transition pressure changes, we have to account for that using the Clapeyron equation. The key here is that the entropy difference between the two phases is related to the slope of the phase boundary on an P-T diagram. The Clapeyron equation says dP/dT equals delta S over delta V. We know the molar entropies, so we can calculate the entropy change per mole. For gray tin, it's about 4 4.14 J/ mol·K, and for white tin, it' s 51.1 9 J/mol·K. So the delta S is roughly 7 J/mol·K. Now, the volume change comes from the densities. Density is mass over volume, so volume is mass over density. Assuming the molar mass is constant, the volume per mo le is inversely proportional to density. Gray tin has a lower density, so it has a larger volume per mole than white tin. The volume change delta V is then R T over delta S times the ratio of densities? Wait, let me think again. Actually, since density is mass over volume, and if we assume the molar mass is the same, v olume per mole is 1 over density. So delta V is approximately 1 over", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 59, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 703, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 7317, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0160_conservative_local.wav", "sha256": "8168d1a0aaf3995f31a9bf98b45c8c1b76debe6172f5fd984f806b5b61f78815", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 60.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.081, 1.148, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.096, 2.162, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.083, 3.149, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.081, 4.152, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.083, 5.151, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.086, 6.155, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.082, 7.154, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.09, 8.165, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.083, 9.153, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.093, 10.166, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 11.0, 11.092, 11.166, 1, 0, 0], [11, 1, 12.0, 12.084, 12.149, 1, 0, 0], [12, 1, 13.0, 13.096, 13.171, 1, 0, 0], [13, 1, 14.0